---
name: notes-to-actions
description: Use when the user pastes raw meeting notes, call transcripts, or a messy set of bullet points and asks for a list of action items, next steps, follow-ups, or "what do we need to do." Also use when they ask to "turn these notes into tasks" or "what came out of this meeting." Do not use for producing a full weekly status report (see weekly-report) or for logging a decision on its own (see decision-log-entry).
notes-to-actions
Turn messy, real-world meeting notes into a clean, reliable action list. The
notes will be informal: half-sentences, fragments, people talking over each
other, things mentioned twice, things that never got resolved. Your job is to
extract only genuine commitments — not to clean up prose, and not to guess.
Steps
Read the whole set of notes first, before extracting anything. Context
earlier in the notes often changes whether something later is a real
commitment or just a re-mention of an idea.
Find every candidate action — anything that sounds like something a
person is going to do.
Classify each candidate as one of:
Action — someone is going to do something. There is a clear task.
Discussed only — the topic came up, was debated, or was mentioned as
a possibility, but nobody committed to doing it.
If you cannot tell which one it is, treat it as Discussed only and
say why in a note. Do not upgrade an ambiguous mention into an action to
make the list look more complete.
For every action, extract three fields: what is to be done, who owns
it, and by when.
Never invent an owner or a deadline. If the notes do not name a
specific owner, the owner is `Unassigned`. If the notes do not name a
specific date or clear timeframe, the deadline is `No date given`. Do not
infer an owner from "whoever usually does this" or a deadline from "soon"
— write down exactly what the notes support and nothing more.
Flag every unassigned or undated action for a human. These are not
errors to quietly patch — they are the point of this Skill. Collect them
in a separate "Needs a decision" list at the end so they cannot be missed.
Output the action list, followed by the "Discussed only" list,
followed by the "Needs a decision" flags.
Output must always contain
A numbered Actions list. Each item: What / Owner / By when.
A Discussed, not actioned list — even if empty, say so explicitly
("Nothing was discussed without an action" is a valid, useful line).
A Needs a decision list of every action missing an owner and/or a
deadline, so a human can resolve them. If this list is empty, say so.
Must never do
Never invent, guess, or infer an owner or deadline that is not stated or
very clearly implied by name in the notes (e.g. "Priya, can you send that
by Friday?" is clear enough; "someone should look at this" is not).
Never merge two different action items into one because they're related.
Never silently drop an ambiguous item — it goes in "Discussed only" or
"Needs a decision," not nowhere.
Never smooth a vague commitment into a firm one. "I'll try to get to it"
is weaker than "I'll have it done Thursday" — reflect that, don't average
it out.
When information is missing
This is the core judgment call in this Skill:
No owner named at all → `Owner: Unassigned` + flag it.
Owner implied but not named ("someone from design") → `Owner: Unassigned (design team member, per notes)` + flag it. Preserve the partial
information rather than discarding it.
No date, or a vague one ("soon", "eventually", "next sprint" with no
sprint dates given) → `By when: No date given` (or, for a vague-but-real
signal like "next sprint," keep the phrase verbatim rather than
converting it to a real date) + flag it.
A commitment with no clear task attached ("Sarah's on it") → only log it
as an action if the preceding sentence makes the task obvious; otherwise
treat as Discussed only and note that the task itself is unclear.
Example — good output
Input notes (excerpt):
> Talked about the onboarding flow being too long. Priya said she'd cut the
> signup form down to 3 fields by end of week. We also discussed maybe
> adding SSO at some point but didn't land on anything. Dan's going to look
> into the drop-off numbers.
Output:
Actions
What: Cut the signup form down to 3 fields. Owner: Priya. By
when: End of week.
What: Look into the drop-off numbers. Owner: Dan. By when:
No date given.
Discussed, not actioned
Adding SSO to onboarding — raised as a possibility, no decision or owner.
Needs a decision
Action 2 (drop-off numbers) has no deadline — confirm with Dan by when.
Why this is good: it kept Priya's real deadline, refused to invent one for
Dan, and correctly left SSO out of the action list since nobody committed to it.
Example — bad output (what not to do)
Same input as above.
Bad output:
Actions
Priya — shorten signup form (this week)
Dan — investigate drop-off numbers (by Friday)
Team — add SSO support
Why this is bad: it invented a Friday deadline for Dan that was never
stated, it promoted the SSO discussion to a committed action with a
fabricated owner ("Team"), and it dropped the distinction between what was
decided and what was merely floated — exactly the two things this Skill
must never do.
